note: from "host.py's parse_json / to_json and interp.py's from_foreign, written in Planes -- C1's centrepiece. JSON at the boundary was called the one IRREDUCIBLE host capability, on the grounds that Planes has no parser. That statement predates grammar/parser.planes, which parses a considerably harder grammar than this one. This file tests the claim: a JSON reader and writer built from the same already-proven capabilities the lexer and parser are built from -- code-point comparison, arithmetic, `for each`, records -- and no language addition. It has no `use` of its own, so nothing above it in the module chain is needed to read JSON."

# ================================================================ what this file produces
#
# `json-parse of text` gives { ok, detail, value } where `value` is a TAGGED
# interp.planes value -- the same { kind, value / items / fields, deriv } shape
# grammar/interp.planes's make-number / make-text / make-list / make-record
# build. That shape is restated below rather than imported: `use` resolves
# downward (interp.planes uses this file, not the reverse), and a record value
# in this model is a list of { key, value } pairs precisely BECAUSE Planes
# cannot build a record with runtime field names. That is what makes a JSON
# object expressible here at all: the keys ride as data, so no dynamic record
# lookup is needed and the static analyser can still see every field access.
#
# `json-text-of` is interp.py's to_json: json.dumps(unwrap(v), indent=2) with
# ensure_ascii. `unwrap` is folded in -- a whole number goes out as an integer
# and a non-whole one as TEXT, so an exact value is never silently rounded on
# the way to a file, exactly as the reference does it.
#
# Two things this file cannot do, both for the same already-ruled reason, and
# both named rather than worked around:
#
#   * JSON's \r, \b, \f and \uXXXX escapes have no Planes spelling. A Planes
#     string literal carries four escapes (\" \\ \n \t) and there is no
#     code-point conversion -- `chr of n` was declined when the string escapes
#     were settled, on the grounds that a magic conversion builtin reintroduces
#     the opacity a closed vocabulary exists to avoid (the same ruling
#     parser.planes cites for parsing a number by hand). So a JSON text
#     carrying one of those four escapes is REFUSED, with a message naming why,
#     rather than silently mangled.
#   * The same absence means a non-ASCII character is written out as ITSELF
#     rather than as \uXXXX. The result is valid JSON that any reader parses to
#     the same value; it is not byte-identical to json.dumps(ensure_ascii=True).

# ================================================================ character classes
#
# Code-point comparison only, the lexer's idiom. `c < " "` is every C0 control
# character, which in JSON is exactly the insignificant whitespace (tab,
# newline, carriage return) -- a control character inside a string is invalid
# JSON, so treating the class as whitespace outside a string is complete.

to json-is-digit of c:
  give c >= "0" and c <= "9"

to json-is-space of c:
  give (c == " ") or (c < " ")

to json-is-punct of c:
  is-brace = (c == "{") or (c == "}")
  is-bracket = (c == "[") or (c == "]")
  give is-brace or is-bracket or (c == ":") or (c == ",")

# One character class covers both numbers and the three bare words (true /
# false / null): a run of these is an "atom", closed and classified when the
# run ends. Deciding number-vs-word per character would need lookahead the
# language does not have; deciding it at the close needs none.
to json-is-atom-char of c:
  is-lower = c >= "a" and c <= "z"
  is-upper = c >= "A" and c <= "Z"
  is-sign = (c == "-") or (c == "+")
  give (json-is-digit of c) or is-lower or is-upper or is-sign or (c == ".")

# ================================================================ the tagged value shape
#
# interp.planes's six constructors, restated (see the note above on why this
# file cannot reach them). Every value carries a `deriv` slot left `nothing`:
# a parsed value's provenance is attached by the caller, at the boundary where
# it entered the program -- the constructors stay derivation-blind here for the
# same reason they do there.

to json-number-value of n:
  give { kind: "number", value: n, deriv: nothing }

to json-text-value of s:
  give { kind: "text", value: s, deriv: nothing }

to json-bool-value of b:
  give { kind: "boolean", value: b, deriv: nothing }

to json-nothing-value:
  give { kind: "nothing", value: nothing, deriv: nothing }

to json-list-value of items:
  give { kind: "list", items: items, deriv: nothing }

to json-record-value of fields:
  give { kind: "record", fields: fields, deriv: nothing }

# ================================================================ small helpers

to json-first of xs:
  result = nothing
  for each x in (first 1 of xs):
    result = x
  give result

to json-no-pending:
  give { kind: "none", text: "" }

# ok / detail live on every state record in this file, so one refusal helper
# serves the tokenizer and the value-builder both.
to json-fail-state of state, detail:
  give state with ok: false, detail: detail

to json-digit-value of c:
  result = 0
  i = 0
  for each d in "0123456789":
    if d == c:
      result = i
    i = i + 1
  give result

# ================================================================ numbers, read exactly
#
# The reference converts a JSON number in two steps: json.loads produces a
# Python int (exact) or float (binary), and from_foreign turns that into an
# exact number -- for a float, via the SHORTEST DECIMAL that round-trips it, so
# a JSON 0.1 becomes one tenth rather than 0.1000000000000000055.
#
# This reads the decimal text directly, which agrees with the reference for
# every JSON number whose decimal form round-trips a float64 -- that is, up to
# 17 significant digits, which is every number JSON is ordinarily written with
# and every number in the corpus. Past that the reference loses digits to the
# float and this does not: the divergence is one-directional and this side is
# the exact one, consistent with the language's own reader (Number.parse reads
# a source `0.1` as one tenth, not as the nearest float, for the same reason).
#
# Whole and fractional parts accumulate as exact integers and the fraction
# becomes frac/10^digits via ordinary `/` -- parser.planes's parse-number-text
# idiom, extended with the sign and exponent JSON allows and Planes source
# does not.

# 10^d for a single digit, so the exponent never needs a loop whose bound is a
# number rather than a collection (Planes iterates collections, not ranges).
to json-pow10-small of d:
  if d == 0:
    give 1
  if d == 1:
    give 10
  if d == 2:
    give 100
  if d == 3:
    give 1000
  if d == 4:
    give 10000
  if d == 5:
    give 100000
  if d == 6:
    give 1000000
  if d == 7:
    give 10000000
  if d == 8:
    give 100000000
  give 1000000000

# 10^(the number those digits spell), folded over the digits themselves:
# reading one more digit takes 10^k to (10^k)^10 * 10^d. The fold is over the
# exponent's TEXT, so its length bounds the work -- no range loop, and no
# recursion, so an exponent costs no interpreter depth.
to json-pow10-of-digits of ds:
  p = 1
  for each c in ds:
    d = json-digit-value of c
    tenth = p * p * p * p * p * p * p * p * p * p
    p = tenth * (json-pow10-small of d)
  give p

to json-number-start:
  give { ok: true, detail: "", value: nothing, neg: false, phase: "int", whole: 0, frac: 0, divisor: 1, exp-neg: false, exp-digits: "", int-count: 0, frac-count: 0, started: false }

to json-number-step of st, c:
  if st.phase == "exp":
    if json-is-digit of c:
      give st with exp-digits: (st.exp-digits + c)
    if (c == "-") and (st.exp-digits == ""):
      give st with exp-neg: true
    if (c == "+") and (st.exp-digits == ""):
      give st
    give json-fail-state of st, ("not a JSON number: '" + c + "' in the exponent")
  if (c == "-") and (not st.started):
    give st with neg: true, started: true
  if c == ".":
    if st.phase == "int":
      give st with phase: "frac", started: true
    give json-fail-state of st, "not a JSON number: a second '.'"
  if (c == "e") or (c == "E"):
    give st with phase: "exp"
  if json-is-digit of c:
    d = json-digit-value of c
    if st.phase == "frac":
      give st with frac: (st.frac * 10 + d), divisor: (st.divisor * 10), frac-count: (st.frac-count + 1), started: true
    give st with whole: (st.whole * 10 + d), int-count: (st.int-count + 1), started: true
  give json-fail-state of st, ("not a JSON number: '" + c + "'")

to json-number-finish of st, raw:
  if not st.ok:
    give { ok: false, detail: st.detail, value: nothing }
  if st.int-count == 0:
    give { ok: false, detail: ("not a JSON value: '" + raw + "'"), value: nothing }
  if (st.phase == "frac") and (st.frac-count == 0):
    give { ok: false, detail: ("not a JSON number: '" + raw + "' has no digits after the '.'"), value: nothing }
  if (st.phase == "exp") and (st.exp-digits == ""):
    give { ok: false, detail: ("not a JSON number: '" + raw + "' has no digits in the exponent"), value: nothing }
  magnitude = st.whole + (st.frac / st.divisor)
  scaled = magnitude
  if not (st.exp-digits == ""):
    p = json-pow10-of-digits of st.exp-digits
    if st.exp-neg:
      scaled = magnitude / p
    else:
      scaled = magnitude * p
  if st.neg:
    give { ok: true, detail: "", value: (0 - scaled) }
  give { ok: true, detail: "", value: scaled }

to json-number-of-text of raw:
  st = json-number-start
  for each c in raw:
    if st.ok:
      st = json-number-step of st, c
  give json-number-finish of st, raw

# ================================================================ tokenizing
#
# A streaming fold over the source's code points, carrying one `pending` record
# for whatever token is mid-flight -- lexer.planes's shape, with a smaller
# vocabulary. No lookahead: a pending atom or string is closed by the character
# that fails to extend it, and that character is then re-offered to the start
# state.

to json-emit-token of st, tok:
  give st with out: (st.out plus tok)

to json-step-string of st, c:
  if c == "\"":
    cleared = st with pending: (json-no-pending)
    give json-emit-token of cleared, { kind: "string", text: st.pending.text, value: 0 }
  if c == "\\":
    give st with pending: { kind: "string-escape", text: st.pending.text }
  give st with pending: { kind: "string", text: (st.pending.text + c) }

# The four JSON escapes with a Planes spelling, plus the solidus (which JSON
# permits escaped and Planes writes plainly). The other four -- \r, \b, \f and
# \uXXXX -- are REFUSED by name: see the note at the top of this file. A refusal
# names the fix, as every error in this language is expected to.
to json-step-escape of st, c:
  t = st.pending.text
  if c == "\"":
    give st with pending: { kind: "string", text: (t + "\"") }
  if c == "\\":
    give st with pending: { kind: "string", text: (t + "\\") }
  if c == "/":
    give st with pending: { kind: "string", text: (t + "/") }
  if c == "n":
    give st with pending: { kind: "string", text: (t + "\n") }
  if c == "t":
    give st with pending: { kind: "string", text: (t + "\t") }
  give json-fail-state of st, (json-escape-refusal of c)

to json-escape-refusal of c:
  opening = "JSON escape '\\" + c + "' has no Planes spelling"
  middle = " -- a Planes string carries four escapes (\\\" \\\\ \\n \\t) and no code-point escape"
  give opening + middle + "; send the character itself instead of escaping it"

to json-close-atom of st:
  t = st.pending.text
  cleared = st with pending: (json-no-pending)
  if t == "true":
    give json-emit-token of cleared, { kind: "true", text: t, value: 0 }
  if t == "false":
    give json-emit-token of cleared, { kind: "false", text: t, value: 0 }
  if t == "null":
    give json-emit-token of cleared, { kind: "null", text: t, value: 0 }
  n = json-number-of-text of t
  if not n.ok:
    give json-fail-state of st, n.detail
  give json-emit-token of cleared, { kind: "number", text: t, value: n.value }

to json-tok-start of st, c:
  if c == "\"":
    give st with pending: { kind: "string", text: "" }
  if json-is-space of c:
    give st
  if json-is-punct of c:
    give json-emit-token of st, { kind: "punct", text: c, value: 0 }
  if json-is-atom-char of c:
    give st with pending: { kind: "atom", text: c }
  give json-fail-state of st, ("unexpected character in JSON: '" + c + "'")

to json-tok-step of st, c:
  p = st.pending
  if p.kind == "string":
    give json-step-string of st, c
  if p.kind == "string-escape":
    give json-step-escape of st, c
  if p.kind == "atom":
    if json-is-atom-char of c:
      give st with pending: { kind: "atom", text: (p.text + c) }
    closed = json-close-atom of st
    if not closed.ok:
      give closed
    give json-tok-start of closed, c
  give json-tok-start of st, c

to json-tok-finish of st:
  if not st.ok:
    give st
  if st.pending.kind == "atom":
    give json-close-atom of st
  if st.pending.kind == "string":
    give json-fail-state of st, "unterminated JSON string: no closing quote"
  if st.pending.kind == "string-escape":
    give json-fail-state of st, "unterminated JSON string: the text ends inside an escape"
  give st

to json-tokens of src:
  st = { ok: true, detail: "", out: [], pending: (json-no-pending) }
  for each c in src:
    if st.ok:
      st = json-tok-step of st, c
  give json-tok-finish of st

# ================================================================ building the value
#
# A shift/reduce fold over the tokens, carrying an explicit stack of open
# containers. NOT recursive descent: nesting costs a stack entry rather than an
# interpreter frame, so a deeply nested document costs no depth at all -- the
# thing that matters when this runs three levels of interpretation down. The
# recursion ceiling is spent on the WRITER (below), where the output's shape
# follows the value's, and not here.
#
# A frame is one open container: an array accumulating `items`, or an object
# accumulating `fields` with the key it is waiting to pair. Both shapes carry
# both fields so `with` never has to add one.
#
# The mode is what the next token may be. "value-or-close" and "key-or-close"
# are the states right after an opening bracket, where the matching close is
# still legal -- that one distinction is what makes `[]` and `{}` fall out
# without a special case while `[1,]` is still refused.

to json-array-frame:
  give { kind: "array", items: [], fields: [], key: "" }

to json-object-frame:
  give { kind: "object", items: [], fields: [], key: "" }

to json-emit-value of st, v:
  if (count of st.stack) == 0:
    give st with value: v, mode: "after", done: true
  top = json-first of st.stack
  others = rest of st.stack
  if top.kind == "array":
    give st with stack: ([top with items: (top.items plus v)] + others), mode: "after"
  give st with stack: ([top with fields: (top.fields plus { key: top.key, value: v })] + others), mode: "after"

to json-close-frame of st, expected:
  if (count of st.stack) == 0:
    give json-fail-state of st, "unexpected close in JSON: nothing is open"
  top = json-first of st.stack
  if not (top.kind == expected):
    give json-fail-state of st, ("mismatched close in JSON: the open container is an " + top.kind)
  popped = st with stack: (rest of st.stack)
  if top.kind == "array":
    give json-emit-value of popped, (json-list-value of top.items)
  give json-emit-value of popped, (json-record-value of top.fields)

to json-step-value of st, t, close-ok:
  k = t.kind
  if k == "punct":
    if t.text == "[":
      give st with stack: ([json-array-frame] + st.stack), mode: "value-or-close"
    if t.text == "{":
      give st with stack: ([json-object-frame] + st.stack), mode: "key-or-close"
    if (t.text == "]") and close-ok:
      give json-close-frame of st, "array"
    give json-fail-state of st, ("expected a JSON value, found '" + t.text + "'")
  if k == "string":
    give json-emit-value of st, (json-text-value of t.text)
  if k == "number":
    give json-emit-value of st, (json-number-value of t.value)
  if k == "true":
    give json-emit-value of st, (json-bool-value of true)
  if k == "false":
    give json-emit-value of st, (json-bool-value of false)
  give json-emit-value of st, (json-nothing-value)

to json-step-key of st, t, close-ok:
  if t.kind == "string":
    top = json-first of st.stack
    give st with stack: ([top with key: t.text] + (rest of st.stack)), mode: "colon"
  if (t.kind == "punct") and (t.text == "}") and close-ok:
    give json-close-frame of st, "object"
  give json-fail-state of st, "expected a JSON field name in double quotes"

to json-step-colon of st, t:
  if (t.kind == "punct") and (t.text == ":"):
    give st with mode: "value"
  give json-fail-state of st, "expected ':' after a JSON field name"

to json-step-after of st, t:
  if not (t.kind == "punct"):
    give json-fail-state of st, "expected ',' or a closing bracket after a JSON value"
  if (count of st.stack) == 0:
    give json-fail-state of st, ("trailing content after the JSON value: '" + t.text + "'")
  top = json-first of st.stack
  if t.text == ",":
    if top.kind == "array":
      give st with mode: "value"
    give st with mode: "key"
  if t.text == "]":
    give json-close-frame of st, "array"
  if t.text == "}":
    give json-close-frame of st, "object"
  give json-fail-state of st, ("expected ',' or a closing bracket, found '" + t.text + "'")

to json-parse-step of st, t:
  if st.mode == "colon":
    give json-step-colon of st, t
  if st.mode == "key":
    give json-step-key of st, t, false
  if st.mode == "key-or-close":
    give json-step-key of st, t, true
  if st.mode == "after":
    give json-step-after of st, t
  give json-step-value of st, t, (st.mode == "value-or-close")

to json-parse-finish of st:
  if not st.ok:
    give { ok: false, detail: st.detail, value: nothing }
  if not ((count of st.stack) == 0):
    give { ok: false, detail: "unterminated JSON value: a bracket is still open", value: nothing }
  if not st.done:
    give { ok: false, detail: "no JSON value found", value: nothing }
  give { ok: true, detail: "", value: st.value }

to json-parse-tokens of toks:
  st = { ok: true, detail: "", mode: "value", stack: [], value: nothing, done: false }
  for each t in toks:
    if st.ok:
      st = json-parse-step of st, t
  give json-parse-finish of st

# The whole reader: text in, { ok, detail, value } out, with `value` a tagged
# interp.planes value. A refusal is a value, never a raise -- the caller (the
# `ask` boundary) falls back to the response's text exactly as the reference
# does when json.loads raises.
to json-parse of src:
  toks = json-tokens of src
  if not toks.ok:
    give { ok: false, detail: toks.detail, value: nothing }
  give json-parse-tokens of toks.out

# ================================================================ writing
#
# interp.py's to_json: json.dumps(unwrap(v), indent=2). The `unwrap` is folded
# in: a whole number goes out as an integer and a non-whole one as its exact
# TEXT (so 1/3 lands in the file as "~0.333333333333" and is not silently
# rounded), which is why no float is ever written and the writer needs no
# float formatting at all.
#
# The indent is carried as the pad STRING rather than a level number, so a
# level costs one concatenation and no range loop. This is the one recursive
# walk in this file -- the output's shape follows the value's, as
# interp.planes's own canonical-of-value already does -- so nesting depth here
# costs interpreter depth. The measured ceiling is in REPORT_HOST_BOUNDARY.md.

to json-escape-char of c:
  if c == "\"":
    give "\\\""
  if c == "\\":
    give "\\\\"
  if c == "\n":
    give "\\n"
  if c == "\t":
    give "\\t"
  give c

to json-escape of s:
  result = ""
  for each c in s:
    result = result + (json-escape-char of c)
  give result

to json-quote of s:
  give "\"" + (json-escape of s) + "\""

to json-render-list of items, pad:
  if (count of items) == 0:
    give "[]"
  inner = pad + "  "
  body = ""
  seen = false
  for each item in items:
    piece = inner + (json-render-value of item, inner)
    if seen:
      body = body + ",\n" + piece
    else:
      body = piece
      seen = true
  give "[\n" + body + "\n" + pad + "]"

to json-render-fields of fields, pad:
  if (count of fields) == 0:
    give "{}"
  inner = pad + "  "
  body = ""
  seen = false
  for each f in fields:
    piece = inner + (json-quote of f.key) + ": " + (json-render-value of f.value, inner)
    if seen:
      body = body + ",\n" + piece
    else:
      body = piece
      seen = true
  give "{\n" + body + "\n" + pad + "}"

to json-render-value of v, pad:
  k = v.kind
  if k == "nothing":
    give "null"
  if k == "boolean":
    if v.value:
      give "true"
    give "false"
  if k == "number":
    if (whole of v.value) == v.value:
      give text of v.value
    give json-quote of (text of v.value)
  if k == "text":
    give json-quote of v.value
  if k == "list":
    give json-render-list of v.items, pad
  if k == "record":
    give json-render-fields of v.fields, pad
  fail "cannot write a value of kind '" + v.kind + "' as JSON\n  JSON holds numbers, text, yes/no, nothing, lists and records; convert the value to one of those first" as cannot-write-json

to json-text-of of v:
  give json-render-value of v, ""
